# Medium-chain-length PHA module preflight — 2026-11-07

## Duplicate and existing-work conclusion

No matching active audit PR was found in live repository PR searches for `mcl` and `polyhydroxyalkanoate`, supplemented by the latest100 open PRs. Human MCL1 hits are unrelated. Prior relevant work is merged: [PR2585](https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/2585) introduced this module and the four-gene review on2026-08-28; [PR2050](https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/2050) covered optional PhaG monomer routing; [PR4795](https://github.com/ai4curation/ai-gene-review/pull/4795) audited sidecars. A taxon/evidence/family audit would extend that merged work, not duplicate an open task. Search snapshots are in this directory; this is a point-in-time conclusion.

Root checkout module is DRAFT. Four local PSEPK reviews are DRAFT: phaG/O85207 (2rows), phaA/PhaC1/Q88D25 (3), phaC/PhaC2/Q88D23 (3), phaZ/Q88D24 (3):11 total rows. The gene names need role-aware handling: phaA here is PhaC1, not the short-chain-PHB thiolase, and phaZ's raw UniProt name is phaB. No other matching phaC1/C2/Z/G gene reviews were found. Existing histories cover phaG/phaC/phaZ including the2026-10-11 sidecar audit; no dedicated module-history directory or phaA history was found. The local module git history traces through an omnibus merge; the explicit prior PR provides the more informative provenance.

Three actual PANTHER families occur in the target proteins, and none has a local family review: PTHR36837 (PhaC1/C2, SF5), PTHR43433 (PhaZ, SF5), PTHR43194 (PhaG, SF5). The latter two are broad hydrolase families; PhaG's SF5 label mentions pimeloyl-ACP methyl-ester esterase. Neither a label nor generic alpha/beta-hydrolase membership grants PHA chemistry. The module currently uses InterPro IPR011287 for classII PhaC and IPR011942 for intracellular PhaZ; phylogenetic scope still needs actual family/tree inspection.

## Recommended initial member panel

1. **Keep and complete the four KT2440 reviews**: Q88D25/PP_5003 PhaC1, Q88D23/PP_5005 PhaC2, Q88D24/PP_5004 PhaZ, O85207/PP_1408 PhaG. Distinguish KT2440 from KCTC1639, strainU and KT2442 in their supporting papers. The existing polymerase reviews cite several of those other strains; they cannot establish a target-specific rank of the two KT2440 synthases.
2. **Add the GPo1 core trio** after normal seeding: P26494 PhaC1, P26496 PhaC2 and P26495 PhaZ. Official UniProt directly links all three to GPo1, depositM58445 and primary1989978. Current official organism naming is Ectopseudomonas oleovorans, whereas later papers call GPo1 Pseudomonas putida/formerly P.oleovorans; preserve strain identity rather than treating the names as independent biological panels. Primary1989978 genetically separates synthesis/degradation and does not establish a universal PhaC1-versus-PhaC2 preference. Primary11133449 adds native GPo1 turnover/complementation, distinct from stress-survival consequences.
3. **Add PAO1 PhaG Q51553/PA0730 as an optional-route comparator** if the complete primary10713430 can be read. The reviewed official record explicitly links this gene to that primary and PAO1. This is a partial route exemplar, not a complete synthesis-and-mobilization panel. Do not automatically add Q9KJH8: its official source is an unpublished sequence submission from ATCC29347/TF4-1L, not an equivalent directly characterized PhaG activity source.

This yields a practical eight-gene initial panel: four existing and four new, representing two complete core systems plus one optional-route comparator. Extension candidates should be conditional:

- **PAO1 PhaC2 G3XCZ9/PA5058** is an informative additional synthase once the construct bridge for primary10952003 is checked. Official ENA X66592 identifies PAO1 and its560aa C2 translation exactly matches current G3XCZ9. The same deposit's557aa PhaC1 differs from current559aa G3XCV5/PA5056: do not call those sequences identical. The full sequence comparison is in pao1-sequence-bridge.json.
- **PAO1 depolymerase G3XD91/PA5057** exactly matches the285aa historical X66592 translation, but both source deposit and current gene name say phaD, while the protein description says depolymerase. Keep this distinct from the later PhaD transcriptional regulator and resolve primary nomenclature/assay scope before creating a new gene review. A complete PAO1 turnover panel is not established merely by the adjacent genes.
- **Pseudomonas chlororaphis PCL1606** (primary37863020) supports a native phaG perturbation and polymer accumulation, but its C1/Z/C2 roles are described as predicted. Polymer monomer composition and exact reviewed proteins need checking. Root-survival/biocontrol phenotypes do not fill a missing direct PhaZ step.
- **P.putida U/CECT4848** (primary20937103) provides unusually direct concomitant synthesis/degradation evidence, but exact accessions are unresolved here. Keep it as a reaction-coupling source until a sequence bridge is established.

## Candidate reviews assessed

- [38845815, Current trends in medium-chain-length polyhydroxyalkanoates (2024)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11151071/): introduction and producer/substrate sections read in full through the web and subsequently acquired via EuropePMC XML. Useful for the C6–C14 versus C3–C5 distinction and for identifying non-pseudomonad producer claims. Production/composition evidence alone does not establish the classII intracellular-turnover mechanism. Its wider Bacillus/Streptomyces/Enterobacter examples require primary confirmation, not automatic module membership.
- [Make it or break it (2025 issue; online2024), DOI10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4): full publisher text acquired; classification, substrate-specificity and depolymerase sections assessed. Useful for classI–IV and intracellular/extracellular distinctions. Its own discussion notes broader substrate ranges than the simple class label predicts; do not make classI universally incapable of incorporating an mcl monomer. Bibliographic platform calls it Original Paper, but the text is a review. No PMID was found in the scoped EuropePMC query.
- [28425176, PHA-associated phasins as heterogeneous multipurpose proteins (2017)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5658603/): full EuropePMC XML acquired and the mcl-PHA/PhaF/PhaI section read. Supports granule organization as a real functional context without making phasins another required chemical conversion. It also points to growth-dependent effects on polymerase activity.
- [19270094, Carbonosomes (2009)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19270094/) and [24329995, New insights in granule formation (2014)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24329995/) are useful backup reviews.2009 full-text attempts hit a challenge/500; these are not counted as full-text-reviewed here. PHB-focused conclusions must not be transferred wholesale to mcl-PHA.

## Priority primary evidence and boundaries

- [1989978 (1991)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1989978/): accessible abstract plus official source-linked GPo1 protein records. Positive genetically separated synthase and depolymerase roles, with no clear polymerase-specificity difference in those experiments. Full text is an early priority.
- [11133449 (2001)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC92552/): publisher web result supports GPo1 PhaZ mutant/complementation and polymer turnover. The direct requests acquisition returned a browser challenge; do not mistake its165-byte text for a full article.
- [10952003 (2000)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10952003/): abstract directly reports purified P.aeruginosa PhaC1 and PhaC2 and in-vitro poly(3-hydroxydecanoate) synthesis. Strong chemistry source, but exact construct/strain transfer still needs full methods.
- [17170116 (2007)](https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M608119200): existing protected cache is abstract-only; publisher/searchable full source identifies KT2442 as a KT2440 derivative, with recombinant E.coli expression. This supports intracellular mcl-PhaZ chemistry, not identical native physiology between KT2440 and KT2442.
- [41055782 (2025)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12504323/): newly discovered priority source, full XML acquired. Its title says KT2440 but methods/Table1 identify KT2442 DNA and the older pPAZ3 construct. Use the wild-type biochemical/context evidence and acknowledge the construct origin. Do not relabel purified-protein experiments native KT2440 flux. Its comparison with extracellular GK13 enzyme is useful for localization and substrate gates.
- [20937103 (2010)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2959000/): existing full cache read for the granule/activity findings. P.putida U has concomitant polymerization and depolymerization; these are opposing arms, not a compulsory temporal switch. The paper's enzyme preparations do not settle KT2440 isoenzyme division of labor.
- [9727022 (1998)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9727022/) and [35338129 (2022)](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-29218-3): reassess optional PhaG routing. The original assay includes CoA-to-ACP transfer, whereas2022 heterologous metabolic evidence favors a transacylase interpretation. Neither alone proves every native reaction edge or a universal PP_0763 requirement. Keep the prior explicit uncertainty until the full source comparison is complete.

## Audit decisions to preserve

The core boundary is externally supplied (R)-3-hydroxyacyl-CoA → intracellular mcl-PHA → mobilized products. PhaC1 and PhaC2 are alternative synthases for the same role, not two sequential obligatory steps. Requiring PhaC plus PhaZ for the *combined synthesis-and-mobilization profile* must not imply PhaZ is required for polymer accumulation; a depolymerase-deficient cell can still synthesize and accumulate PHA. PhaG and beta-oxidation routes are alternative precursor contexts.

A capacity oracle should separately test synthesis and full turnover, reject a generic acyltransferase or hydrolase, distinguish intracellular from extracellular PhaZ, and reject a PHB-only short-chain system as evidence for the specified mcl profile. That is a boundary of evidence/profile matching, not a claim that every classI enzyme or every non-pseudomonad organism is biologically incapable of mcl chemistry. Family labels, taxon alone and polymer staining cannot satisfy the reaction-specific gates.

## Access and preservation

No curated file, existing publication/provider cache or worktree was changed. All outputs are confined to this new ignored preflight directory. The HTTP200 PMC challenge bodies are marked as unsuccessful acquisitions; full2024 and2025 sources were recovered through EuropePMC, and the2017 phasin review likewise. Normal publication fetching should wait for the dedicated worktree. This is a preflight proposal, not completed gene/family curation or an assay-validated capacity model.
